---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { browserEngines, dialects, engine, labels, recipes, site, url } from '../lib/site';

const variants = recipes.filter((r) => dialects.some((d) => r.queries[d]?.variant)).length;
const unsupported = recipes.filter((r) => Object.keys(r.unsupported).length).length;
---

<Base title="About" description="What “runs” means here, how CI checks every recipe on real servers, which engine versions, and who made it.">
  <article class="about">
    <header class="page-head">
      <span class="label">About</span>
      <h1>What <em>“runs”</em> means.</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Most SQL cheat sheets show a query and a result that someone typed by hand, once. Here a result is a file, <code>expected.json</code>, and the
        tick next to an engine means the query was sent to that engine and returned exactly those rows.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is checked</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Each of the {recipes.length} recipes is a folder: <code>recipe.md</code> for the prose, <code>query.sql</code> for the standard query,
          <code>query.&lt;dialect&gt;.sql</code> where an engine needs its own idiom ({variants} recipes have one), and <code>expected.json</code> for the
          rows. Recipes that share data start from one of the <a href={url('fixtures/')}>three fixtures</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          A Python harness, <code>cook</code>, runs every recipe on every engine. SQLite and DuckDB run in-process. PostgreSQL and MySQL are real servers,
          started as services in GitHub Actions; each run gets a fresh schema or database that is dropped afterwards.
        </li>
        <li>
          Rows are compared after a small normalisation: numbers to six decimals, timestamps as <code>YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS</code>, JSON text parsed. A
          recipe that marks <code>ordered: true</code> must return rows in that order; otherwise order is free.
        </li>
        <li>
          CI runs the matrix on three pairs of server versions (PostgreSQL 16, 17 and 18 with MySQL 8.0, 8.4 and 9), and a weekly job tries the newest
          releases. A page whose query fails on any engine doesn't ship.
        </li>
        <li>
          {unsupported} {unsupported === 1 ? 'recipe says' : 'recipes say'} <code>unsupported</code> for an engine that has no way to express it. That shows
          as a struck-through tab with the reason, and as – in the <a href={url('matrix/')}>matrix</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The engines on this page</h2>
      <p>
        The ticks on every page come from the run that built it: {dialects.map(engine).join(', ')}. The <b>Run in your browser</b> button loads a real
        engine compiled to WebAssembly, so you can edit a query and see the rows change:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          dialects.map((d) => (
            <li>
              <b>{labels[d]}</b>:{' '}
              {browserEngines[d]
                ? `${browserEngines[d]!.lib}, about ${browserEngines[d]!.size} on first use, cached by the browser afterwards.`
                : 'no WebAssembly build exists, so the panel says so. Its ticks come from CI alone.'}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        The browser engines are the same database code as the servers, but not the same version as CI, and the live comparison with the expected rows
        uses the same normalisation the harness does.
      </p>

      <h2>License and author</h2>
      <p>
        MIT, for the recipes, the harness and this site. Made by {site.author} (<a href="https://github.com/FlutterSmith">FlutterSmith</a> on GitHub),
        who also keeps <a href="https://fluttersmith.github.io/tips/">tips</a> and <a href="https://fluttersmith.github.io/py-snippets/">py-snippets</a>.
        The source, the tests and the site are on <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a>; a recipe that is wrong on some engine version is a bug, and an issue
        with the version is enough.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
